course: dictionary
lesson: 1217
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and «bug» is a good example. The words around it, its context, tell you which meaning the writer means. «I've got a tummy bug» means a germ that makes you mildly ill. «Fix a bug» in a computer program means a mistake to correct.
explain: Read the whole sentence before you decide. «Stop bugging me» uses bugging as a verb, which means annoying someone again and again, not an insect. Try the swap: «Stop annoying me» still makes sense, so you know you have the right meaning.
fact: A word with more than one meaning is called a word with several meanings.
fact: «Bug» can mean a small insect or other tiny crawling creature, like a beetle or an ant.
fact: «Bug» can mean a germ that makes you mildly ill, like a «stomach bug».
fact: «Bug» can mean a mistake in a computer program; finding and fixing it is called debugging.
fact: As a verb, «bug» means to annoy someone again and again, and «bugging» is its -ing form.
exercise: «A robot follows its steps exactly; a mistake in the steps is called a bug.» What does bug mean here? | A tiny crawling beetle | A cold or stomach germ | A sudden strong interest | *A mistake in the steps a robot follows
exercise: «Scientists keep the word bug for one group of insects with a straw-like mouth.» Which creatures do scientists call true bugs? | Worms that live in soil | Beetles, which chew their food | *Insects with a straw-like mouth that suck | Spiders with eight legs
exercise: «Four coders who each fix 12 bugs a day fix 48 bugs a day.» What do the coders fix? | Broken windows | Bicycles | Holes in the road | *Mistakes in computer programs
test: Mum says, «I've got a tummy bug, so I'm staying in bed.» What does bug mean? | *A germ that makes you mildly ill | A hidden microphone | A sudden strong interest | A small insect
test: Sam says, «He has the reading bug; he reads every night.» What does bug mean? | A tiny beetle | A mistake in a computer | To annoy someone again and again | *A sudden strong interest
exam: «They put a bug under the table to hear the secret meeting.» What does bug mean here? | A germ that makes you ill | *A tiny hidden microphone used to listen secretly | A mistake in a program | A small insect
